1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,530
Two people edit the same file at the same time.

2
00:00:03,530 --> 00:00:05,142
One saves over the other,

3
00:00:05,142 --> 00:00:06,947
and an hour of work is gone.

4
00:00:06,947 --> 00:00:09,332
Branches exist so that never happens.

5
00:00:09,882 --> 00:00:12,302
Last lesson you controlled your commits.

6
00:00:12,302 --> 00:00:15,508
Today you learn to work in parallel: create a branch,

7
00:00:15,508 --> 00:00:16,355
merge it back,

8
00:00:16,355 --> 00:00:18,351
and fix a conflict without panic.

9
00:00:18,901 --> 00:00:19,766
Three skills.

10
00:00:19,766 --> 00:00:20,432
Branching,

11
00:00:20,432 --> 00:00:22,428
so your experiments stay safe.

12
00:00:22,428 --> 00:00:22,960
Merging,

13
00:00:22,960 --> 00:00:24,823
so finished work comes home.

14
00:00:24,823 --> 00:00:25,754
And conflicts,

15
00:00:25,754 --> 00:00:29,547
the scary message that turns out to be a five minute job.

16
00:00:30,097 --> 00:00:32,913
As always, there is a lab with a checker at the end.

17
00:00:34,963 --> 00:00:35,339
First,

18
00:00:35,339 --> 00:00:35,904
the idea.

19
00:00:35,904 --> 00:00:38,349
A commit is a snapshot of your project.

20
00:00:38,349 --> 00:00:41,171
Each commit points back to the one before it.

21
00:00:41,721 --> 00:00:44,835
A branch is just a name that points at one commit.

22
00:00:44,835 --> 00:00:45,956
Nothing is copied.

23
00:00:45,956 --> 00:00:49,444
That is why branches in Git are instant and almost free.

24
00:00:49,994 --> 00:00:51,870
The default branch is called main.

25
00:00:51,870 --> 00:00:52,753
When you commit,

26
00:00:52,753 --> 00:00:55,732
the branch you are on moves forward to the new commit.

27
00:00:56,282 --> 00:00:59,562
And head is a pointer to the branch you are on right now.

28
00:00:59,562 --> 00:01:00,483
Switch branches,

29
00:01:00,483 --> 00:01:01,346
and head moves,

30
00:01:01,346 --> 00:01:03,130
and your files change to match.

31
00:01:03,680 --> 00:01:06,252
So a branch is a safe place to try things.

32
00:01:06,252 --> 00:01:07,722
If the experiment fails,

33
00:01:07,722 --> 00:01:09,069
you delete the branch,

34
00:01:09,069 --> 00:01:10,294
and main never knew.

35
00:01:10,844 --> 00:01:16,028
One habit before we start: git status first. It always tells you which branch you are on.

36
00:01:18,078 --> 00:01:20,012
When work on a branch is finished,

37
00:01:20,012 --> 00:01:21,890
you merge it into another branch,

38
00:01:21,890 --> 00:01:22,629
usually main.

39
00:01:22,629 --> 00:01:24,051
There are three outcomes.

40
00:01:24,601 --> 00:01:25,776
One: fast forward.

41
00:01:25,776 --> 00:01:28,256
Main has not moved since you branched.

42
00:01:28,256 --> 00:01:30,931
Git just slides the main pointer forward.

43
00:01:30,931 --> 00:01:31,845
No new commit,

44
00:01:31,845 --> 00:01:32,367
no fuss.

45
00:01:32,917 --> 00:01:34,066
Two: a true merge.

46
00:01:34,066 --> 00:01:36,045
Both branches have new commits,

47
00:01:36,045 --> 00:01:37,577
but in different places.

48
00:01:37,577 --> 00:01:41,535
Git combines them and creates a merge commit with two parents.

49
00:01:42,085 --> 00:01:43,210
Three: a conflict.

50
00:01:43,210 --> 00:01:45,522
Both branches changed the same lines.

51
00:01:45,522 --> 00:01:47,521
Git cannot guess which you want,

52
00:01:47,521 --> 00:01:49,083
so it stops and asks you.

53
00:01:49,633 --> 00:01:52,463
Two out of three outcomes need nothing from you.

54
00:01:52,463 --> 00:01:53,289
And the third,

55
00:01:53,289 --> 00:01:54,527
you are about to see,

56
00:01:54,527 --> 00:01:55,883
is just editing a file.

57
00:01:57,933 --> 00:02:03,288
Let us see it live. This project has two commits on main. Go in. Type: CD, site.

58
00:02:03,838 --> 00:02:06,270
List the branches. Type: git branch.

59
00:02:06,820 --> 00:02:07,542
One branch,

60
00:02:07,542 --> 00:02:07,870
main,

61
00:02:07,870 --> 00:02:10,036
and the star shows where you are.

62
00:02:10,036 --> 00:02:13,252
Create a new branch and switch to it in one step.

63
00:02:13,252 --> 00:02:14,368
Type: git switch,

64
00:02:14,368 --> 00:02:14,828
dash C,

65
00:02:14,828 --> 00:02:15,353
feature.

66
00:02:15,903 --> 00:02:18,847
Switched to a new branch. Check. Type: git branch.

67
00:02:19,397 --> 00:02:21,340
The star moved to feature.

68
00:02:21,340 --> 00:02:22,985
Now do some work here.

69
00:02:22,985 --> 00:02:24,704
Add a line to the page.

70
00:02:24,704 --> 00:02:25,526
Type: echo,

71
00:02:25,526 --> 00:02:25,974
quote,

72
00:02:25,974 --> 00:02:27,170
Owner colon Sam,

73
00:02:27,170 --> 00:02:27,619
quote,

74
00:02:27,619 --> 00:02:28,441
two arrows,

75
00:02:28,441 --> 00:02:29,786
index dot H T M L.

76
00:02:30,336 --> 00:02:31,021
Commit it.

77
00:02:31,021 --> 00:02:35,197
The dash A option stages changes to files Git already tracks,

78
00:02:35,197 --> 00:02:37,183
and dash M gives the message.

79
00:02:37,183 --> 00:02:38,346
Type: git commit,

80
00:02:38,346 --> 00:02:38,963
dash A M,

81
00:02:38,963 --> 00:02:39,373
quote,

82
00:02:39,373 --> 00:02:40,400
Add owner line,

83
00:02:40,400 --> 00:02:40,811
quote.

84
00:02:41,361 --> 00:02:42,117
One commit,

85
00:02:42,117 --> 00:02:43,147
on this branch.

86
00:02:43,147 --> 00:02:45,140
Now see the shape of history.

87
00:02:45,140 --> 00:02:46,101
Type: git log,

88
00:02:46,101 --> 00:02:47,407
dash dash one line,

89
00:02:47,407 --> 00:02:48,506
dash dash graph,

90
00:02:48,506 --> 00:02:49,468
dash dash all.

91
00:02:50,018 --> 00:02:55,116
The feature branch is one commit ahead of main. Now go back. Type: git switch, main.

92
00:02:55,666 --> 00:02:59,272
Look at the page. Type: cat, index dot H T M L.

93
00:02:59,822 --> 00:03:01,285
The owner line is gone!

94
00:03:01,285 --> 00:03:02,239
It is not lost.

95
00:03:02,239 --> 00:03:04,211
It lives on the feature branch,

96
00:03:04,211 --> 00:03:06,819
and Git swapped your files to match main.

97
00:03:06,819 --> 00:03:07,583
Switch back,

98
00:03:07,583 --> 00:03:08,537
and it returns.

99
00:03:08,537 --> 00:03:10,382
That is what isolation means.

100
00:03:12,432 --> 00:03:13,841
Now bring work home.

101
00:03:13,841 --> 00:03:14,969
In this project,

102
00:03:14,969 --> 00:03:17,295
feature and main have both moved.

103
00:03:17,295 --> 00:03:17,717
Go in.

104
00:03:17,717 --> 00:03:18,352
Type: CD,

105
00:03:18,352 --> 00:03:18,704
site.

106
00:03:19,254 --> 00:03:21,043
Look at the picture first.

107
00:03:21,043 --> 00:03:22,006
Type: git log,

108
00:03:22,006 --> 00:03:23,313
dash dash one line,

109
00:03:23,313 --> 00:03:24,414
dash dash graph,

110
00:03:24,414 --> 00:03:25,377
dash dash all.

111
00:03:25,927 --> 00:03:30,944
Two lines of work that split from the same commit: the stylesheet on feature,

112
00:03:30,944 --> 00:03:32,639
and an owner line on main.

113
00:03:32,639 --> 00:03:33,681
You are on main,

114
00:03:33,681 --> 00:03:35,310
so merge feature into it.

115
00:03:35,310 --> 00:03:38,373
The no edit option accepts the default message.

116
00:03:38,373 --> 00:03:39,416
Type: git merge,

117
00:03:39,416 --> 00:03:40,915
dash dash no dash edit,

118
00:03:40,915 --> 00:03:41,436
feature.

119
00:03:41,986 --> 00:03:42,745
Merge made.

120
00:03:42,745 --> 00:03:43,573
No conflict,

121
00:03:43,573 --> 00:03:46,610
because the changes touched different files.

122
00:03:46,610 --> 00:03:47,991
Look at the history.

123
00:03:47,991 --> 00:03:48,957
Type: git log,

124
00:03:48,957 --> 00:03:50,268
dash dash one line,

125
00:03:50,268 --> 00:03:51,373
dash dash graph.

126
00:03:51,923 --> 00:03:54,936
The two lines of work join at a merge commit.

127
00:03:54,936 --> 00:03:56,811
The branch has done its job,

128
00:03:56,811 --> 00:03:57,682
so delete it.

129
00:03:57,682 --> 00:04:01,834
The lowercase D only deletes branches that are already merged,

130
00:04:01,834 --> 00:04:02,772
so it is safe.

131
00:04:02,772 --> 00:04:03,910
Type: git branch,

132
00:04:03,910 --> 00:04:04,379
dash D,

133
00:04:04,379 --> 00:04:04,915
feature.

134
00:04:05,465 --> 00:04:08,451
Deleted. Check what is left. Type: git branch.

135
00:04:09,001 --> 00:04:13,033
Only main. The work is merged, and the history keeps the full story.

136
00:04:15,083 --> 00:04:16,864
Now the part everyone fears.

137
00:04:16,864 --> 00:04:18,262
When Git cannot merge,

138
00:04:18,262 --> 00:04:20,679
it writes both versions into the file,

139
00:04:20,679 --> 00:04:21,696
between markers.

140
00:04:22,246 --> 00:04:23,662
Seven less than signs,

141
00:04:23,662 --> 00:04:24,306
then head,

142
00:04:24,306 --> 00:04:25,271
mark your side,

143
00:04:25,271 --> 00:04:26,687
the branch you are on.

144
00:04:26,687 --> 00:04:29,390
Seven equals signs split the two versions.

145
00:04:29,390 --> 00:04:31,256
And seven greater than signs,

146
00:04:31,256 --> 00:04:32,608
with the branch name,

147
00:04:32,608 --> 00:04:33,830
end the other side.

148
00:04:34,380 --> 00:04:36,071
Your job has four steps.

149
00:04:36,071 --> 00:04:39,946
One: open the file and decide what the right result is.

150
00:04:39,946 --> 00:04:42,411
Two: delete all three marker lines,

151
00:04:42,411 --> 00:04:44,313
and keep the right content.

152
00:04:44,313 --> 00:04:46,004
Three: git add the file.

153
00:04:46,004 --> 00:04:47,202
Four: git commit.

154
00:04:47,752 --> 00:04:49,362
And if you are not ready,

155
00:04:49,362 --> 00:04:53,548
git merge dash dash abort puts everything back exactly as it was.

156
00:04:53,548 --> 00:04:54,578
Nothing is lost.

157
00:04:56,628 --> 00:04:58,241
Let us do it for real.

158
00:04:58,241 --> 00:04:59,414
In this project,

159
00:04:59,414 --> 00:05:02,053
both branches changed the port line.

160
00:05:02,053 --> 00:05:02,492
Go in.

161
00:05:02,492 --> 00:05:03,152
Type: CD,

162
00:05:03,152 --> 00:05:03,519
site.

163
00:05:04,069 --> 00:05:07,759
Merge. Type: git merge, dash dash no dash edit, feature.

164
00:05:08,309 --> 00:05:08,971
Conflict,

165
00:05:08,971 --> 00:05:09,558
content,

166
00:05:09,558 --> 00:05:10,734
in app dot conf.

167
00:05:10,734 --> 00:05:12,423
Automatic merge failed.

168
00:05:12,423 --> 00:05:15,142
Do not panic: Git is waiting for you.

169
00:05:15,142 --> 00:05:16,978
Ask for the short status.

170
00:05:16,978 --> 00:05:18,227
Type: git status,

171
00:05:18,227 --> 00:05:18,741
dash S.

172
00:05:19,291 --> 00:05:21,040
Two U letters: unmerged,

173
00:05:21,040 --> 00:05:22,643
both sides changed it.

174
00:05:22,643 --> 00:05:23,081
First,

175
00:05:23,081 --> 00:05:24,319
the escape hatch.

176
00:05:24,319 --> 00:05:25,485
Type: git merge,

177
00:05:25,485 --> 00:05:26,651
dash dash abort.

178
00:05:27,201 --> 00:05:30,593
Back to normal. Check. Type: git status, dash S.

179
00:05:31,143 --> 00:05:32,395
Nothing to report,

180
00:05:32,395 --> 00:05:34,552
as if the merge never happened.

181
00:05:34,552 --> 00:05:35,525
Now try again,

182
00:05:35,525 --> 00:05:36,151
for real.

183
00:05:36,151 --> 00:05:37,264
Type: git merge,

184
00:05:37,264 --> 00:05:38,864
dash dash no dash edit,

185
00:05:38,864 --> 00:05:39,421
feature.

186
00:05:39,971 --> 00:05:44,152
The same conflict. Open the file. Type: cat, app dot conf.

187
00:05:44,702 --> 00:05:46,107
There are the markers.

188
00:05:46,107 --> 00:05:46,682
Our side,

189
00:05:46,682 --> 00:05:48,343
port nine thousand ninety.

190
00:05:48,343 --> 00:05:49,045
Their side,

191
00:05:49,045 --> 00:05:50,770
port eight thousand eighty.

192
00:05:50,770 --> 00:05:53,388
The team agreed on eight thousand eighty,

193
00:05:53,388 --> 00:05:55,241
and the timeout line is fine.

194
00:05:55,241 --> 00:05:56,582
Write the final file.

195
00:05:56,582 --> 00:05:57,412
Type: printf,

196
00:05:57,412 --> 00:05:59,073
the text in single quotes,

197
00:05:59,073 --> 00:06:01,245
port equals eight thousand eighty,

198
00:06:01,245 --> 00:06:02,011
backslash N,

199
00:06:02,011 --> 00:06:03,288
timeout equals five,

200
00:06:03,288 --> 00:06:04,055
backslash N,

201
00:06:04,055 --> 00:06:05,779
redirect into app dot conf.

202
00:06:06,329 --> 00:06:11,236
No markers left. Tell Git it is resolved. Type: git add, app dot conf.

203
00:06:11,786 --> 00:06:16,053
Now finish the merge. Type: git commit, dash dash no dash edit.

204
00:06:16,603 --> 00:06:22,491
Merge committed. Look at the history. Type: git log, dash dash one line, dash dash graph.

205
00:06:23,041 --> 00:06:23,925
A clean graph,

206
00:06:23,925 --> 00:06:27,208
and a merge commit that records how you resolved it.

207
00:06:27,208 --> 00:06:28,913
A conflict is not an error.

208
00:06:28,913 --> 00:06:30,934
It is Git asking you a question.

209
00:06:33,084 --> 00:06:35,857
Your task, in your own Ubuntu virtual machine.

210
00:06:36,407 --> 00:06:38,918
The lab is lab fifty-nine dot S H,

211
00:06:38,918 --> 00:06:42,683
in the academy slash lab folder of git hub dot com,

212
00:06:42,683 --> 00:06:44,160
slash it dash waves,

213
00:06:44,160 --> 00:06:45,858
slash dev ops dash A I.

214
00:06:45,858 --> 00:06:47,557
Install it like before,

215
00:06:47,557 --> 00:06:48,738
then run lab up.

216
00:06:49,288 --> 00:06:51,288
It builds a repository on main,

217
00:06:51,288 --> 00:06:53,482
with two feature branches waiting.

218
00:06:53,482 --> 00:06:54,708
One merges cleanly.

219
00:06:54,708 --> 00:06:57,096
The other conflicts on the port line.

220
00:06:57,646 --> 00:07:00,206
One: merge feature slash logging into main.

221
00:07:00,756 --> 00:07:03,175
Two: merge feature slash port into main,

222
00:07:03,175 --> 00:07:04,445
resolve the conflict,

223
00:07:04,445 --> 00:07:06,623
and keep port eight thousand eighty.

224
00:07:07,173 --> 00:07:09,370
Three: delete both merged branches.

225
00:07:09,920 --> 00:07:14,933
Four: in answers dot text, write one number: how many merge commits main has now.

226
00:07:15,483 --> 00:07:20,411
Then run lab check. Four pass lines. Before you merge, always run git status.

227
00:07:22,461 --> 00:07:24,445
Six points, and you need all six.

228
00:07:24,995 --> 00:07:26,830
The checker shows four pass lines.

229
00:07:27,380 --> 00:07:30,452
You were on main when you merged, and checked with git status.

230
00:07:31,002 --> 00:07:33,946
No conflict markers remain in app dot conf.

231
00:07:34,496 --> 00:07:38,336
You can explain when Git needs a merge commit and when it fast forwards.

232
00:07:38,886 --> 00:07:41,211
The number in answers dot text is correct.

233
00:07:41,761 --> 00:07:43,404
You ran lab down to clean up.

234
00:07:43,954 --> 00:07:45,575
To pass, you need all six.

235
00:07:47,625 --> 00:07:50,462
Your turn, in your own Ubuntu virtual machine.

236
00:07:51,012 --> 00:07:53,231
One: install the lab, and run lab up.

237
00:07:53,781 --> 00:07:55,509
Two: merge the logging branch.

238
00:07:56,059 --> 00:07:59,707
Three: merge the port branch, and fix the conflict by hand.

239
00:08:00,257 --> 00:08:02,028
Four: delete both branches.

240
00:08:02,578 --> 00:08:06,247
Five: count the merge commits, write the number, and run lab check.

241
00:08:06,797 --> 00:08:08,269
Six: run lab down.

242
00:08:08,819 --> 00:08:10,654
Pause the video, and begin now.

243
00:08:26,204 --> 00:08:27,079
Quick check.

244
00:08:27,629 --> 00:08:28,994
What is a branch, really?

245
00:08:29,544 --> 00:08:31,315
Which merge needs no new commit?

246
00:08:31,865 --> 00:08:33,635
What do the three marker lines mean?

247
00:08:34,185 --> 00:08:36,745
How do you back out of a merge you are not ready for?

248
00:08:37,295 --> 00:08:43,333
Objective covered: M C zero seven, objective three, branches, merging, and conflicts.

249
00:08:43,883 --> 00:08:45,952
Pause the video, and answer out loud.

250
00:08:56,502 --> 00:08:59,766
Next: rebasing, a cleaner way to bring work together.

251
00:09:00,316 --> 00:09:03,644
If this helped, like, share and subscribe, so you do not miss it.
